  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A skateboarder moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-6,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the skateboarder has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the skateboarder moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(1)^{3}-3(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 0 & =1-3-6+C \\\\ C & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the skateboarder at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+8.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the skateboarder is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-6t+8=(t-1)({t}^{2}-2t-8)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094076/assets/c86cdd70316b0bdb620a9a3224823be70069328e0aac83d2c0add2b925323423.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$",
